Solid-state organic–inorganic hybrid perovskite solar cells are one of the most important recent discoveries in the field of photovoltaics.1-4 The first descriptions of solar cells that employed methylammonium lead iodide (MAPbI3) perovskite nanocrystals as the light absorbing material in a liquid electrolyte, such as an iodine/triiodide redox couple were reported by Miyasaka et al.5 Since those studies, Snaith6 and Park7 independently employed $^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}$ -tetrakis $(\mathrm{N},\mathrm{N}^{\prime}$ -di-pmethoxyphenylamine)- $^{.9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD) as an organic hole transporting material (HTM) in place of the liquid electrolyte, which improved the power conversion efficiency (PCE, with an increase from 3.8 to $9.7\mathrm{-}10.9\%\mathrm{\Omega}$ ) and stability. In general, perovskite solar cells are composed of a counter electrode / HTM / perovskite / electron transport layer (ETL) on a fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) coated glass. The active surface area may be
## ACS Nano
increased by forming a perovskite layer on a mesoscopic scaffold layer, such as a layer composed of ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ or $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles. Recently, Soek reported a maximum power conversion efficiency that exceeded $20\%$ in solar cells employing a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesoscopic scaffold layer and the formamidinium lead iodide (FAPbI3) perovskites.8 Devices prepared without a mesoscopic scaffold layer (e.g., a planar perovskite cell) have attracted significant attention for their simple device architecture and fabrication procedures. The PCEs of these devices, however, are around $12\%$ , as reported by Snaith and Yang, independently,9,10 apparently lower than the values obtained from devices with a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesoscopic scaffold layer.
Efforts have been applied toward improving the PCE and stability of the devices by developing advanced materials (perovskites11-18 and HTMs19-27) while also examining the roles of the components and their operating principles.28-32 However, The importance of the ETL has not yet extensively explored. The ETL plays an important role in extracting electrons from the perovskite layer and blocking recombination between electrons in the FTO and holes in the perovskite layer. An excessively thick ETL can minimize recombination, although the electron flow may be hampered due to the high series resistance. A few solar cell structures have been prepared without an ETL, although the resulting PCEs tend to be low compared to planar perovskite cells prepared with an ETL.33,34
Recently, several research groups have developed new ETL materials, including graphene $\mathrm{\TiO}_{2}$ nanocomposites, $^{35}\:\:Z\mathrm{nO},^{36,37}$ and yttrium-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{(Y-TiO}_{2})$ ,38 that provide efficient electron transport and permit room temperature device fabrication. Dense $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs have not been extensively investigated, although they are the ETLs most widely used in the field. Titanium isopropoxide (TTIP) solutions spin-coated onto FTO (denoted to $\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}}$ ) provide stable characteristics under harsh chemical conditions and excellent electrical properties.39-42
## ACS Nano
Here, we describe the relationship between the morphology of a conventional $\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}}$ layer on FTO and the electron transport properties. The spin-coating process formed a highly irregular thick $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ layer on top of the rough FTO layer, resulting in the reduced interfacial area at the perovskites / $\mathrm{\backslash{S-TiO}}_{2}$ / FTO layer interface and the poor electron extraction. These problems were addressed by implementing a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL based on a simple sputtering and anodization method (denoted $\mathrm{\bfA}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ). The resulting $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{Ti}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ ETL yielded well-defined nanostructured morphology with excellent physical properties, including single crystalline properties, the absence of pinholes, a uniform film thickness, enhanced transmittance, and strong connections between the FTO and ETL.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## ACS Nano
Figure 1 shows high-resolution scanning electron microscopy (HR-SEM) top and crosssectional images of the $\mathrm{\bfS}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer prepared with different thicknesses $20{-}80~\mathrm{nm}$ ) on the FTO substrate. The rough FTO glass (see the inset of Figure 1a) became smooth as the thickness of the $\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}}$ layer increased. The $20~\mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}}$ layer retained the rough top surface of the FTO glass, although numerous discontinuous $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ areas (marked as pinholes in Figure 1a) appeared. The corresponding vertical image (Figure 1d) revealed that the bare FTO surface was exposed and could form direct contact with the perovskite layer. Pinholes were observed in the $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ layer (Figure 1b), and they were absent from the ca. $80\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ layer (Figure 1c). The surface smoothness increased with the $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ layer thickness on the rough FTO glass, as determined by AFM imaging (Figure S1a and S1b). The root-mean-square (rms) roughness of the $\mathrm{\bfS}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ sample was clearly lower than that of the bare FTO glass due to overfilling of the valleys on the FTO surface with $\mathrm{{S-TiO}}_{2}$ , as is evident in the vertical images (Figs. 1e and 1f). For instance, the $80\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}}$ layer displayed a vertically non-uniform morphology at thickness values of $45\mathrm{-}160\mathrm{nm}$ .
Figure 2. Photocurrent-photovoltage $\left(J\ –\nabla\right)$ properties of the planar perovskite solar cells prepared using $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ ETLs with various thicknesses, measured under AM 1.5 solar illumination.
The irregular thick $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ layer (and the resulting smooth surface) or the presence of pinholes may have significantly impacted the device performance. For instance, if the $\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}}$ were too thin or the perovskite layer directly contacted the FTO layer, electrons in the FTO layer easily recombined with holes due to the lack of hole blocking ( $\textcircled{2}$ and $\textcircled{3}$ in Figure 2a). On the other hand, the electron flow from the perovskite layer to the thick $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ region was ineffective due to the high series resistance, and the electrons recombined with the holes ( $\textcircled{4}$ in Figure 2a). The resulting smooth surface decreased the interfacial surface area between the perovskite and S$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers, resulting in a low device performance, especially in the planar perovskite solar cells.
## ACS Nano
The irregular ETL morphology significantly influenced the device performance, as observed in the photocurrent−photovoltage $(J-V)$ curves of the devices prepared with various $\mathrm{{S-TiO}}_{2}$ ETL thicknesses (Figure 2 and Table 1). First, a device without an ETL was prepared such that the perovskite layer directly contacted the FTO surface. This structure was analogous to a structure with a gigantic pinhole. The PCE was $2.0\%$ , and the photovoltaic parameters were poor (the short circuit current density $(J_{S C})$ was $14.0\ \mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , the open circuit voltage ( $\left(V_{O C}\right)$ was $0.49\mathrm{~V~}$ , and the fill factor $(F F)$ was 0.29). In addition to inefficient electron extraction, the low $F F$ resulted mainly from recombination between the photoinduced electrons and holes ( $\textcircled{3}$ in Figure 2a), which again decreased $V_{O C}$ and $J_{S C}$ .43 Next, devices were fabricated with 20 or $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ ETLs to improve the electron extraction efficiency and minimize direct contact between the perovskite layer and the FTO surface, thereby decreasing the number of pinholes, as shown in Figs. 1a and 1b. The PCE values improved dramatically to $12.5\%$ for the $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{{S-TiO}}_{2}$ layer. These values were equivalent to those reported independently by Snaith and Yang in studies of planer devices.9,10 The $F F$ values increased to 0.45 and 0.67 for 20 and $40\mathrm{nm}$ thick S$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers, respectively, indicating that electron extraction properly occurred, and direct contact between the perovskite layer and the FTO surface was minimized. Further increases in the S$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL thickness could increase the length of the electron transport pathway. The electrons were trapped in the thick $\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}}$ ETL and recombined with holes ( $\textcircled{4}$ in Figure 2a). The $V_{O C}$ and $F F$ values in the device prepared with $60\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ layers were $0.98{\mathrm{~V~}}$ and 0.57, smaller than those ( $1.03{\mathrm{~V~}}$ and 0.67) obtained from the $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ layers. These values again decreased as the $\mathrm{\bfS}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Ti}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ thickness increased $\mathrm{80~nm},$ ), affording $V_{O C}$ and $F F$ values of $0.92~V$ and 0.26. These results revealed the importance of the $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ thickness and the absence of direct contact (pinholes) between the perovskite layer and the rough FTO surface. These results also suggested that vertical uniformity in the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer, along with complete coverage of the rough FTO surface, could maximize electron extraction and minimize recombination to enhance the photovoltaic performance in planer devices.
Figure 3. Surface HR-SEM images of bare FTO glass (a), Ti-deposited FTO glass (b), and anodized $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)$ ) on FTO glass (c). (d) Cross-sectional HR-SEM image of $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ on FTO glass. (e) Transmittance spectra of the bare FTO, $\mathrm{\mathbf{A}}{-}\mathrm{Ti}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and spin-coated $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ glass (S$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{.}$ ). The thickness of the deposited $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer was $40\mathrm{nm}$ . The inset shows photographs of the substrates after each treatment.
A $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer with vertical uniformity and a high surface roughness was achieved on the FTO surface using a proposed method (Figure 3a-d). This process involved three simple steps: (i) sputtering a Ti layer onto the FTO surface, (ii) anodization, and (iii) thermal annealing. The thickness of the Ti layer was controlled by the sputtering time (ca. 10 min for $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ thickness). After the Ti deposition, a mild anodization process at $5\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ in ethylene glycol solution containing $0.25~\mathrm{wt\%}$ NH4F and $0.3\ \mathrm{vol\%}$ distilled water converted the opaque Ti layer to a transparent and amorphous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer at room temperature for 5 min (see Figure S2 for effects of the anodization time and voltage on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface morphology). The anodizing conditions are described in greater detail in the ESI†.44 The amorphous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer was thermally annealed at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ to produce a crystalline (anatase) phase.45
The $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer completely retained the rough surface of the FTO glass. The thickness of the $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer on the rough surface of the FTO glass was highly uniform, as shown in the surface and vertical SEM images (Figure 3c and 3d), respectively. The AFM analysis confirmed the uniform surface morphologies in the films (Figure S1a and S1c). The rms roughness value of the $\mathrm{\mathbf{A}}–\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film was almost identical to that of the bare FTO. Therefore, the high effective contact area between at the $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and FTO and an efficient electron transport properties are expected at the $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{\mathbf{A}}–\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer deposited on the FTO glass exhibited a better transparency than the corresponding $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ layer on the FTO glass and was comparable to the bare FTO glass, as shown in Figure 3e and the inset of Figure 3e. Notice that the $60\mathrm{nm}$ thick A$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer displayed a transmittance similar to that of the $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}}$ layer (Figure S3). The outstanding transparency of the $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{Ti}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ layer was ascribed to the uniform film morphology, and was expected to improve the light harvesting.
