HR-TEM images of the $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ layers indicated the difference of physical contact between the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer and the rough FTO surface. Figure 4f shows an empty area within the deep sharp valley regions of the FTO, indicating the absence of physical contact between the S$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and the FTO surface. A liquid to solid $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ anatase crystal phase transformation and the concomitant volume shrinkage could be induced in the deep sharp valley regions. Composite (carbon and Ti in Figure 4i) and carbon (Figure 4j) mapping images of the $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ layer after filling viscous carbon liquid, obtained from electron energy loss spectroscopy (EELS) analysis, clearly revealed an empty area between the $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ and the FTO layers (see Figure S6 for more images).
Meanwhile, the $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{Ti}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ layer displayed robustly constructed highly ordered single crystals at the FTO surface, as identified from the absence of carbon (C) atoms in the composite mapping image of the $\mathrm{\mathbf{A}}–\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer (Figure 4d). These morphologies were confirmed using an oxygen (O) atom mapping image of the $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer on the surface of the FTO glass (Figure 4e). Oxygen atoms in this layer corresponded to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and FTO units. This image also confirmed that the Ti layer was completely converted to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ during anodization. $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ conversion from Ti is also identified by secondary ion mass spectrometry (SIMS) as shown in Figure S7. Compared with Ti depth profile, $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ exhibits increased $^{16}\mathrm{O}$ signal, and the enhanced $^{16}\mathrm{O}$ intensity of $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with increased film thickness (as shown in SIMS depth profiles of $40\mathrm{nm}$ and $80\mathrm{nm\A-TiO}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ ) supports $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ formation. We also confirmed that the surface morphology and the electrical property $(2.0\sim2.1$ ohm/sq by the 4 point probe resistivity measurements) of the FTO were not changed at all during the anodization process due to its high chemical resistance (Figure S8).46 Additionally, we also fabricated the electron only device and confirmed the improved mobility at $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ electron only device (Figure S9, from $3.09\times10^{-5}~\mathrm{{cm}^{2}/V S}$ to $1.42\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~cm}^{2}/\mathrm{VS})$ .  In addition to improved light absorption and an increase in the effective contact area between at the $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and perovskite layer, as mentioned above, fast electron transport and a long electron lifetime in the single-crystalline anatase $\mathrm{TiO\it^{47}}$ and defect-free physical contact between the A$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and FTO surfaces could improve the photovoltaic performances of devices prepared with an $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL.
