The advantages of the $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{Ti}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ ETL in comparison to the $\mathrm{\bfS}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL were made apparent in tests of a planer perovskite solar cell (Table 1). Figure 5 shows the photocurrent–photovoltage $(J{-}V)$ properties of planar perovskite solar cells prepared with $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{Ti}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ ETLs of various thicknesses $(20-80~\mathrm{nm})$ . A device employing a $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer exhibited the best photovoltaic performance, with a PCE of $15.2\%$ , a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $20.5\ \mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , a $F F$ of 0.70, and a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.06\mathrm{~V~}$ . The PCE of $15.2\%$ was apparently greater than the best values (around $12\%$ ) in the planer perovskite solar cells as reported by Snaith and Yang, independently.9,10 As the thickness of the $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer increased $(40\to60\to80\mathrm{nm}$ ), the defect sites generated due to the increased series resistance48,49 became dominant and increased recombination ( $\textcircled{4}$ in Figure 2a), as observed in the device prepared using an $80\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ ETL (Figure 2). The $80\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{\mathbf{A}}–\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ exhibited the worst performance $(\mathrm{PCE}=8.5\%$ ), with a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}=18.3~\mathrm{mA/cm}^{2}$ , $F F=0.49$ , and $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=0.95\:\mathrm{V}$ . The device prepared with a $20~\mathrm{{nm}}$ thick $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ yielded a $F F$ of 0.57, much lower than that obtained in the device prepared with a $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer (0.70) although the thickness uniformity was excellent (Figure S10). This result indicated that a thickness of 20 nm was insufficient to prevent the recombination reaction ( $\textcircled{3}$ in Figure 2a).
