uld be extracted from excited perovskites into the $\mathrm{A}{-}\mathrm{Ti}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ETL.
## CONCLUSIONS
Conventional $\mathrm{\S-TiO}_{2}$ ETLs produce highly irregular film morphologies on rough FTO surfaces, and they exert a bad influence on the performances of planar perovskite devices. The morphological problems in the $\mathrm{\bfS}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs were addressed here by alternative ETL fabrication method based on sputtering and anodization. The resultant $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ displayed well-defined nanostructure morphology with a uniform film thickness on the rough FTO surface. The unique morphological characteristics of $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{Ti}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ enhanced the transmittance to improve light absorption and increase the contact area between the perovskite and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL. In addition, the $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was single-crystalline in nature and provided defect-free physical contact between the $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and FTO surface, which improved the effective electron extraction and hole blocking properties. In a planar perovskite device, $\mathrm{\mathbf{A}}–\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ increased the PCE by $22\%$ (from 12.5 to $15.2\%$ ) and increased the stabilized maximum power output efficiency by $44\%$ (from 8.9 to $12.8\%$ ) to provide $84.2\%$ of the first scan efficiency. The high stabilized maximum power output efficiency of $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ reveals that the $\mathrm{\mathbf{A}}–\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ device functioned well under realistic device operation conditions. The good performance of the device prepared using $\mathrm{\mathbf{A}}–\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was attributed to the excellent electron extraction and hole blocking properties of $\mathrm{\mathbf{A}}{-}\mathrm{Ti}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Spectroscopy and rectifying analysis results clearly demonstrated the outstanding electron transport capabilities of $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . These results revealed that the performance of the ETL was predominantly affected by the morphological properties of the ETL. Our ETL fabrication method provides important insights into achieving ideal ETL morphologies that remedy the drawbacks observed in conventional spin-coated ETLs.
## METHODS
Preparation of $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathbf{TiO}_{2}$ on FTO glass. The $\mathbf{A}{-}\mathrm{Ti}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ was prepared by a potentiostatic anoidzation method. Anodization was carried out using a Ti deposited fluorine-doped $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ (FTO) conducting glass (Pilkington TEC 7, ${7}\Omega/\Sigma\dot{}$ ) as a working electrode and a carbon plate as a counter electrode. Before Ti deposition, the FTO glass was rinsed with detergent, distilled water, ethanol, and acetone. The Ti layer was deposited on cleaned FTO glass by a radio frequency sputter (AMAT E5500 at national institute for nanomaterials technology (NINT) in POSTECH).
Anodization was conducted at $5\mathrm{~V~}$ for $5~\mathrm{min}$ in ethylene glycol solution containing $0.25\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ and $0.3\mathrm{\vol\%}$ distilled water at room temperature. During anodizing, the solvent was continuously stirred to prevent fluctuation of current. The resulting sample was cleaned using ethanol to remove the acidic salts and debris, followed by thermal annealing at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ .
Device Fabrication. The planar perovskite solar cells were fabricated on FTO glass. Some parts of FTO were treated with 2 M HCl solution containing zinc powder to make insulator regions. The resulting FTO was washed using detergent, acetone. The spin-coated $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ compact layer $\mathrm{(S-TiO_{2})}$ ) was prepared by following method. A solution of $0.5~\mathrm{mM}$ titanium isopropoxide (TTIP) in ethanol and $40~\mathrm{mM}$ HCl solution in ethanol were slowly mixed together. The mixed solution was drop-wised on the substrate and spin-coated at $2000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for 1 min. The $\mathrm{{S-TiO}}_{2}$ - coated substrates were heated at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . The perovskite precursor was prepared following the reported method.53 Perovskite precursor solution was spin-coated on the substrate at $2000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $60\mathrm{~s~}$ and then crystallized by heating at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 5 min in the glove box. The 2,2′,7,7′-tetrakis(N,N′-di-p-methoxyphenylamine)-9,9′-spirobifluorene (Spiro-MeOTAD) (Merck KGaA) in chlorobenzene ( $180~\mathrm{mg/1~ml)}$ solution with Li-bis(tri-uoromethanesulfonyl) imide (Li-TFSI) and tBP (tert-butyl pyridine) was spin-coated on the perovskite layer. Finally, $100\mathrm{nm}$ silver electrode was vacuum-deposited on the spiro-MeOTAD overlayer.
Characterization. Field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM, Hitachi S 4800) was utilized for morphological and structural characterization. High resolution transmission electron microscopy (HR-TEM) analyses were made with a JEM-2200F electron microscope with an accelerating voltage of $200\mathrm{kV}$ . Photovoltaic performances of the devices were measured under air AM 1.5G illumination of $100\mathrm{\mW/cm}^{2}$ (Oriel $1~\mathrm{kW}$ solar simulator), which was calibrated with a KG5 filter-covered silicon photovoltaic solar cell traceable to the national renewable energy laboratory (NREL). A mask was used to define the device illumination area of $0.09\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ to minimize photocurrent generation from the edge of the electrodes. Secondary Ion Mass Spectrometry (SIMS) measurement was done using the SIMS system (IMS-6f (Physical Electronics USA, Chanhassen, MN)). Beam energy was $15\ \mathrm{keV}$ using $\mathrm{CS^{+}}$ ions as the primary ions and raster was 250 microns (depth) and 100 microns (image). Base pressure was always below $2\mathrm{x}10^{9}$ torr. Space charge limited current (SCLC) was also measured in the same condition but in dark condition. The IPCE spectra were measured under a constant white light bias of approximately $5\mathrm{\mW/cm}^{2}$ supplied by an array of white light emitting diodes using a power source with a monochromator (Zahner GmbH) and a multimeter chopped at approximately $4\:\mathrm{Hz}$ . The atomic force microscope (AFM) (Dimension 3100, VEECO) was operated in tapping mode to acquire images of the surfaces of films. Transmittance and photoluminescence (PL) spectra were obtained employing an OPTIZEN POP UV-vis-near-IR single beam spectrophotometer and Jasco FP-6500 spectrometer, respectively. Time-resolved PL decay measurements were obtained using a FluoTime 300, PicoQuant GmbH. The samples were photoexcited using a 507 nm laser head (LDH-P-C-510, PicoQuant GmbH) pulsed at 10 MHz substrate samples, with a pulse duration of 117 ps and a fluence of $30\mathrm{nJ}/\mathrm{cm}^{2},$ /pulse. The PL was collected from a high resolution monochromator and a hybrid photomultiplier detector (PMA Hybrid 40, PicoQuant GmbH). The sheet resistance of FTO surface was measured with the 4-point probe method (KEITHLEY 2400 sourcemeter).
